  "description": "Construct a pandas DataFrame from dictionary data and extract a designated column as a Python list.\n\nColumn selection in pandas retrieves Series structures backed by contiguous NumPy arrays.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\text{DataFrame}[\\text{column\\_name}].\\operatorname{tolist}()\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Instantiate pandas DataFrame from record dictionary\n2. Index the specified column name\n3. Convert series into a native Python list\n\n### Returns\nList of extracted column values.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Column Selection\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Column Selection** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nColumn selection in pandas retrieves Series structures backed by contiguous NumPy arrays. In this problem, the goal is to: construct a pandas dataframe from dictionary data and extract a designated column as a python list.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\text{DataFrame}[\\text{column\\_name}].\\operatorname{tolist}()\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Instantiate pandas DataFrame from record dictionary\n2. **Phase 2**: Index the specified column name\n3. **Phase 3**: Convert series into a native Python list\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: data = {\"name\": [\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\"], \"age\": [25, 30, 35]}, column_name = \"age\"\n\nOutput: [25, 30, 35]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_column_selection under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `pandas_column_selection`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
